UPDATE decision_nodes SET data = '{"type":"question","module":3,"title":"Septic Shock: Refractory Hypotension / MAP Goal Not Met / Next Step","body":"**Septic shock, refractory hypotension, MAP goal not met on norepinephrine: what is the next step?** Add vasopressin to norepinephrine when norepinephrine requirements are escalating, rather than relying only on further norepinephrine escalation. SSC 2021 described usual addition at norepinephrine 0.25-0.5 mcg/kg/min; this is a practice range, not a requirement to delay vasopressin. [2][26]\n\n**[Vasopressin](#/drug/vasopressin/septic shock)** 0.03-0.04 units/min (fixed dose, non-titratable)\n• Reduces NE requirement, **lower AF risk** (RR 0.77) [24]\n• VASST + VANISH trials: no mortality benefit but NE-sparing [12][13]\n• Best as early second agent — add before escalating NE to high doses [23]\n\n**[Epinephrine](#/drug/epinephrine/septic shock infusion)** 0.01-0.5 mcg/kg/min\n• Alternative first-line for bradycardia or cardiac dysfunction [23]\n• CAT trial: outcomes similar to norepinephrine [23]\n• **Increases lactate** — rising lactate on epi is expected and often a positive prognostic sign [23]\n\n**MAP still below target despite norepinephrine + vasopressin:** add epinephrine and reassess perfusion and the cause of shock. [26]\n\n**When to add steroids:** the SSC 2021 practical trigger is an ongoing norepinephrine or epinephrine requirement of at least 0.25 mcg/kg/min for at least 4 hours to maintain target MAP. Hydrocortisone is an adjunct, not a replacement for the next vasopressor. SSC 2026 suggests IV corticosteroids for septic shock without making that older threshold a mandatory cutoff; lower dose or shorter duration alone does not rule out steroids. [2][26] [Steroid timing and dose](#/node/sepsis-steroids)\n\nWhat is the clinical scenario?","citation":[2,12,13,23,24,26],"options":[{"label":"Vasodilatory Shock — Add Vasopressin","description":"Wide pulse pressure, warm extremities, low DBP, adequate HR","next":"sepsis-map-target"},{"label":"Low Cardiac Output / Bradycardia","description":"Narrow pulse pressure, cool extremities, HR inappropriately low","next":"sepsis-inotrope"},{"label":"Refractory to Multiple Agents","description":"On high-dose NE +/- vasopressin, still hypotensive","next":"sepsis-refractory","urgency":"critical"}],"treatment":{"firstLine":{"drug":"Vasopressin","dose":"0.03-0.04 units/min","route":"IV","frequency":"continuous (fixed)","duration":"non-titratable","notes":"Add early before escalating NE to high doses. Reduces AF risk."},"alternative":{"drug":"Epinephrine","dose":"0.01-0.5 mcg/kg/min","route":"IV","frequency":"continuous","duration":"titrate to MAP ≥65","notes":"Preferred for bradycardia or cardiac dysfunction. Increases lactate (expected)."},"monitoring":"MAP target ≥65 mmHg. Vasopressin is NE-sparing but no mortality benefit (VASST, VANISH)."},"summary":"Septic shock next step for refractory hypotension or MAP goal not met: add vasopressin to escalating norepinephrine; assess hydrocortisone dose and duration trigger separately."}'::jsonb
